(this post is rambly because it's more of an eulogy than a YASD post)

I had this great Rogue going - 88 HP and an AC of -8 (hey, that's a
lot of 8s) probably doesn't sound too great, but that's just what made
him so cool.  He was level 12 and could totally mow down anything that
came his way (he once killed a mumak in two hits, and a gargoyle in
3).  He started out with 14 +0 daggers and wielded them to the bitter,
bitter end (in fact, dagger was the only skill I bothered advancing,
since I hadn't found anything better).

Now this kid, he was from a long, long time ago.  I don't even
remember starting him - I rolled up a rogue a few months ago, took him
down to level 3 and left him there because I got sick of playing a
rogue.  When I picked him up again, I thought it would be over pretty
quick since a moment of temporary stupidity had left him wearing a
cursed -1 cloak and a cursed -2 shield.  He'd gotten impatient trying
on a horde of hill-orcs' armour, by the looks of it.

Well, luckily for him, *I* at least was in a patient mood and cleared
some potions to make holy water with.  While he dipping at a fountain,
a water demon popped out and let him wish for a blessed +2 SDSM.  He
made the holy water, uncursed his cloak and tried on the armour.  It
was blessed, it was +2 and best of all, it was silver! (the last time
I wished for DSM I made a typo and got WDSM instead)

To balance out this ridiculously good luck, Sokoban held an "oR.  I
was annoyed and didn't bother finishing that last level.

Some time later, after taming a pair of dogs and zapping them with his
sokoban /oPoly (I probably should have saved that for polypiling but
good as my luck was I wasn't really expecting to live long enough to
need that) my rogue ended up with a pet water troll and wood golem. 
The wood golem got killed while he wasn't looking but the water troll
survived (actually, he got killed too but came back).  After finding
ad aerium scribbled on the ground, he searched a bit, dropped his gold
and stepped on the teleport trap.  Before he could scoop up all the
gold in the vault, however, he accidentally stepped on a portal and
ended up in Ludios.

His pet troll was not with him.  He had 88 HP.  He had heard the
horror stories.  He was very nervous.  Still, he thought, if things
got too intense he could just go back through the portal.  And with
that mindset he cautiously whacked his way through a roomful of bees,
a treasure zoo, the barracks (what does it mean when you've killed
half the soldiers and hear "the sound of ammunition being readied" 40
times in a row?) and a horde of dragons, collecting along the way 12
cram rations, 44 C-rations and 53 K-rations.  Nice. (he wasn't strong
enough to take them with him, though, so he just left them in a corner
next to his collection of Ludios wands)

One of the highlights of Ludios was standing just outside the door to
the second room, killing anything that came through it.  After
everything was gone I walked into that room and got "Welcome to
David's treasure zoo!" and the only thing in there was this confused
bugbear wandering around.  Heh.

Well, like I said, not even the dragons presented much of a challenge.
 My rogue killed them, ate their corpses (completing his set of
intrinsics) and started a very nice scale collection (green, black,
white).  He was on his way to the treasure vaults when he came across
a golden naga and just *had* to choke on the corpse. (Why?  Why, I ask
you, why?)

Stupid, stupid, stupid, stupid, stupid...I was hoping for a rogue
first ascention, too (much more classy than barbarians or valkyries,
doncha think?).  Well, that was that.  The funny thing was, I hadn't
eaten a bite for at least 100 turns.  Wouldn't that have been long
enough to work off the extra nutrition and make the game warn me about
choking?

Gah, that was so stupid, and it was all my fault too.  The RNG had
been more or less smiling on me for the whole entire game - this is
its idea of a cruel joke, isn't it?

